version: 2
title: How a version-1 kanban works
description: A board whose columns are status and whose rows are derived from dependency edges — what a card opens, how an edit or an Ask goes through the same mutators, what a sourced board may and may not change, and what the calendar lens takes from it.
decisions:
  - key: host
    label: Where is it open?
    detail: The same board draws everywhere; only a host that lets it author shows the controls.
    values:
      - key: surface
        label: The Studio, the desktop app, VS Code
        detail: One surface per document, the preview with `editable={false}` — the board is read here; the file is edited as text.
      - key: authoring
        label: A host that lets it author
        detail: A project's file pane — an `onChange` and no `editable={false}`; columns, tasks and the Ask panel write the file.
  - key: tasks
    label: Where do the tasks come from?
    values:
      - key: own
        label: The file's own `tasks`
      - key: sourced
        label: A `source:` ref
        detail: A `.points` file or a folder of composite `.node`s; the board owns only its edges, durations, dates and provenance.
events:
  - key: always
    label: Always
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Always
    detail: What holds at every moment — the shape of the file, why rows and dates are never authored, and where the engine lives.
    content:
      key: shape
      label: The shape of the file
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: The shape of the file
        description: Columns and tasks are authored; rows and dates are facts about the edges.
        sections:
          - title: Top-level keys
            body: |
              | key | what it is |
              |---|---|
              | `title` | the board's heading |
              | `description` | one line under it |
              | `due` | the day everything must be done by — the anchor backward scheduling chains from |
              | `source` | optional: where the tasks come from (a `.points` ref or a folder of `.node`s) |
              | `columns` | the statuses in order — a string, or `{title, color}` (`label` reads as `title`) |
              | `tasks` | the cards |

              Refs resolve against the board's own folder. A date is `YYYY-MM-DD`: a bare
              `2026-09-09` is read as a date by YAML and kept as the day written; quoted, it is the
              same day. A column's `color` is any CSS colour (`"#3b82f6"`, `tomato`).
          - title: A task
            body: |
              ```yaml
              tasks:
                - key: print-badges          # the stable key `needs:` edges point at; the title when absent
                  title: Print the badge sheets
                  status: Done               # a column TITLE, case-insensitive; unset or unknown = first column
                  file: badges.pdf           # a document beside the board, opened in the card dialog by its kind
                  section: Round 1           # for a .brief: only the first section whose title contains this
                  note: one line under the title
                  body: |                    # the task's own markdown — the dialog's main content
                    What doing this means, in this task's words.
                  needs: [order-badges]      # prerequisites, by key
                  duration: 2                # calendar days (default 1) — a weekend inside a task is part of it
                  due: 2026-09-08            # a cap tighter than the board's
              ```

              A task with neither `title` nor `key` is dropped; a second task with a key already
              seen is dropped, because a duplicate key would fork every edge to it; a `needs`
              entry naming the task itself is dropped.
          - title: Rows are derived
            body: |
              Row one is every task that needs nothing; each later row is what builds on the rows
              above, by longest prerequisite chain (Kahn's order, so a diamond sits as deep as its
              longest path). Edges to unknown keys are ignored. Tasks caught in a cycle cannot be
              layered honestly, so they land together in one final row rather than vanishing. A
              board with no `needs` is one row — the plain kanban it degenerates to. Authoring
              depth separately would let it drift from the edges, so it is computed on every draw.
          - title: Dates are derived
            body: |
              `scheduleTasks` chains backward from the due date: a task ends at the earliest of its
              own `due`, the board's `due`, and the day before its earliest dependent starts, then
              runs `duration` days back from there. A task with no anchor anywhere downstream, or in
              a cycle, is unscheduled rather than given an invented date. The `.calendar` lens draws
              exactly this.
          - title: Where the engine lives
            body: |
              `packages/filekinds/src/lib/kanbanDoc.ts` — `parseKanban`, `dependencyRows`,
              `columnIndexOf`, `dependentsOf`, `scheduleTasks`, `briefSectionSlice`,
              `resolveBoardSource`, `tasksFromPoints`, `tasksFromNodeFolder`, `mergeSourceTasks`;
              its header comment is what `studio-check --spec kanban` prints. The mutators are
              `kanbanEdit.ts` (`addTask`, `updateTask`, `removeTask`, `moveTask`, `addColumn`,
              `renameColumn`, `removeColumn`, `moveColumn`, `setBoardMeta`, `dumpKanban`) and the
              Ask vocabulary `kanbanAsk.ts`. The view is `components/kanban/KanbanBoardView.tsx`
              with `TaskDialog.tsx`, `KanbanEditDialogs.tsx` and `KanbanAsk.tsx`; the checker is `python/studio_kinds/kinds/kanban.py`.
  - key: opened
    label: The file is opened
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Opening
    detail: parseKanban reads the YAML once and never throws; the rows are computed and the columns drawn.
    hint: Say where the tasks come from — a sourced board reads another file before it draws.
    content:
      key: draw
      label: What is drawn
      kind: md
      by: [tasks]
      docs:
        tasks=own: |
          **The parse.** An unparseable file opens as an empty board. Columns keep a `title` and an
          optional `color`; a column with neither `title` nor `label` is nothing to draw. Each task
          keeps the fields the shape lists; `duration` only when a positive number, `due` only when
          a date. A board with no columns draws one column called "Tasks".

          **What is drawn.** The title and description. The column headers, each with its colour
          dot (grey when none). Then one section per dependency row — "Row 1 · nothing blocks
          these", "Row 2 · builds on row 1" — and inside it the columns again, each card standing
          under its status column (`columnIndexOf`: case-insensitive; unset or unknown lands in
          the first column). A card shows the title, the note, an icon for its file, "⇠ N" for
          the prerequisites it needs and "N ⇢" for the tasks that depend on it. No tasks: "No
          tasks yet."

          **The controls.** Only in a host that lets the board author: "+ Column" and "Ask" in the
          header, pencil and trash on each column header, "Add task" under every column. In the
          Studio, the desktop app and VS Code the surface passes `editable={false}`, so none of
          them show and the file is edited as text.
        tasks=sourced: |
          **The parse** is the same; the drawn document is not the file. After the parse
          `resolveBoardSource` reads the ref, resolved against the board's folder:

          - a `.points` file: every point becomes a task — its id the key, its label the title,
            its `status` key the column, its `description` key the body, its note the note;
          - a folder: every `<name>.node` folder that holds a schema half of type `composite` and a
            content half becomes one task keyed by the folder name; every stream is folded to its
            current picture (later arrivals win) and `title`, `status`, `description` and `note`
            land on the task. A node that is not composite, or will not read, is skipped.

          Then `mergeSourceTasks`: the source owns identity, title, status, body and note — a
          stale board-side `status:` can never shadow the stream; a point that has not accreted
          status sits in the first column — and the board owns what only a board knows: `needs`,
          `duration`, `due`, `file`, `section`. Board tasks with no source row are kept whole,
          after the sourced ones. A source that cannot be read degrades to the board's own tasks.

          **What is drawn** is the same board, with "tasks sourced from <ref> — edit them there;
          this board owns its columns and edges" under the title, and "No tasks arrived from the
          source yet." when the merge is empty. "Add task" never shows; the column controls do.
  - key: card-clicked
    label: A card is clicked
    trigger: chosen
    arity: many
    domain: Reading
    detail: The task dialog — facts, edges as jumps, the task's own words, and its document rendered by kind.
    content:
      key: dialog
      label: What the dialog shows
      kind: md
      doc: |
        The title, then — when the board authors — a pencil ("Edit this task") and a trash
        ("Delete this task…"); when the task has a file, a copy-handle button and "Open in
        Nodes". A line of facts: the note; "needs" with one chip per prerequisite and "depended
        on by" with one per dependent, each chip a colour dot, the task's title and its column,
        and a click JUMPS the dialog to that task; "no dependency edges" when there are none.

        Then the body, rendered as markdown, leading. Then, when the task has a file, the
        document under a "Source · <file> · § <section>" bar, rendered by its own kind through
        `DocumentPreview` — a `.brief` sliced first to the first section whose title contains
        `section` (case-insensitively), re-dumped as a one-section brief; no match, or any other
        kind, shows the whole document. "Loading…" until it is read; an unreadable ref shows no
        preview. A task with neither body nor file: "No content authored — the card is the whole
        task." The card clicked also becomes where the Ask panel aims.
  - key: edited
    label: A column or a task is edited
    trigger: chosen
    arity: many
    domain: Authoring
    when: [host=authoring]
    detail: Every dialog goes through kanbanEdit; a refusal is a strip on the board, never a throw; the file is written through the host.
    content:
      key: edit
      label: What each control does
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: What each control does
        description: The dialogs, the mutators behind them, and what reaches the file.
        sections:
          - title: Columns
            body: |
              "+ Column" opens the column dialog (title, colour) and `addColumn` appends it; refused
              when the title is empty or already taken, case-insensitively. The pencil on a header
              opens the same dialog on that column and `renameColumn` retitles or recolours it — the
              tasks standing in it follow. The trash asks: "Delete the column "X"? N tasks in it
              fall back to "First column". This cannot be undone." — `removeColumn` drops their
              `status`. Clicking a header aims the Ask panel at that column.
          - title: Tasks
            body: |
              "Add task" under a column opens the task dialog with that status; the fields are the
              title, the description (the file's `note`), the details (the file's `body`), the
              column, the prerequisites, the file and the section. `addTask` slugs the key from the
              title (lower-case, dashes, at most 48 characters; `-2`, `-3` when taken), drops and
              names any `needs` that is unknown or the task itself, refuses an empty title, an
              unknown column, or a `due` that is not an ISO date. The dialog's pencil opens the
              same dialog on the task and `updateTask` applies the patch — an empty field removes
              it; a new key re-points every edge to it. The trash asks: "Delete "T"? N tasks
              depend on it — those edges are cut. This cannot be undone." — `removeTask` unlinks them.
          - title: A sourced board
            body: |
              Adding, removing and moving tasks are refused: "tasks come from <source> — only
              needs, duration, due, file and section are this board's to change". An update may
              touch only those five; a sourced task with no row yet gets one holding the key and
              them. Columns are always the board's own.
          - title: What reaches the file
            body: |
              Every accepted edit is made to the FILE's document (never the drawn, merged one),
              dumped by `dumpKanban` — `title`, `description`, `due`, `source`, `columns` (a string,
              or `{title, color}`), then each task's `key` and only the fields it has; a title equal
              to the key is left out and the parser restores it — and handed to the host's
              `onChange`. The host's autosave writes it (800 ms debounce, retried after an error)
              and the new text comes back as content, so the board redraws from the file. Comments
              in the YAML do not survive a dump. A refused edit shows in a strip with "dismiss" and
              writes nothing.
  - key: asked
    label: The board is asked to change
    trigger: chosen
    arity: many
    domain: Authoring
    when: [host=authoring]
    detail: One line of instruction, answered with ops from a closed vocabulary and applied through the same mutators.
    content:
      key: ask
      label: What the Ask panel does
      kind: md
      doc: |
        "Ask" shows only when the host has configured the ask worker (the panel checks its
        health on port 9250 and says whether it answers through the API or the CLI, or is
        offline). The panel opens beside the board; a card opened or a column header clicked
        becomes the target it aims at. The model reads a compact listing of the board plus the
        target and answers with `say` and `ops` from `add_task`, `update_task`, `remove_task`,
        `move_task`, `add_column`, `rename_column`, `remove_column`, `move_column`, `set_title`.
        It speaks the user's words for a task's parts — `description` is the file's `note`,
        `details` its `body` — names a task by key, exact title or a handle set with `as` earlier
        in the same reply, and places with `after` ("" for first).

        `applyKanbanOps` runs the ops through `kanbanEdit`, so nothing a model says can produce a
        shape the board could not read; a refusal (an unknown column, a sourced board) becomes a
        skipped line the panel shows. The resulting text goes to the host's `onChange` like a
        dialog edit; the last four turns stay as history, and Undo restores the snapshot taken
        before a turn and marks that turn and the later ones undone.
  - key: changed-on-disk
    label: The file changes on disk
    trigger: imposed
    arity: many
    domain: Reading
    detail: The host re-reads; the board re-parses, re-resolves its source, and keeps the open card if its key survived.
    content:
      key: reread
      label: What survives a re-read
      kind: md
      doc: |
        The desktop watcher (250 ms debounce, `studio:fs-changed`), the web folder entry's server
        events or VS Code's text document deliver the new text; the Studio re-reads unless its
        autosave is dirty, saving or in error, and the board's own saves come back the same way.
        The board re-parses, recomputes the rows and, with a `source`, reads it again. The open
        dialog stays on its key and closes if the key is gone; the Ask target stays. A change to
        the SOURCE file alone is noticed only when this file's text or path changes.
  - key: lensed
    label: Another kind reads it
    trigger: imposed
    arity: many
    domain: Elsewhere
    detail: The board is data to a calendar and a schema.
    content:
      key: elsewhere
      label: Who reads a board
      kind: md
      doc: |
        - A `.calendar` names it as `board:` and draws `scheduleTasks` on a month grid — the same
          merged tasks this board shows, since it resolves the source the same way — and opens the
          same task dialog on a chip.
        - A `.schema` of `type: kanban` describes the pipeline a structured node's content follows;
          a plain `.kanban` file never carries a schema.
        - A journey's fanout stage projects its columns as a board of its own (`FanKanban`), but
          that is not this file.
  - key: checked
    label: studio-check runs on it
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Writing
    detail: One file in, one verdict out — the parser never refuses, so the checker names everything it silently dropped or defaulted.
    content:
      key: checker
      label: What the checker does
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: What the checker does
        description: The order it runs in, and what it leaves alone.
        sections:
          - title: In order
            body: |
              1. Parse the YAML — a parse error is the only problem reported.
              2. `kanbanProblems` — the field table's required rows, and every fallback the lenient
                 parser takes: no `title`, no `columns` (or an empty list, or a column without a
                 title), no `tasks` (`tasks: []` is an empty board and passes), a task with no `key`
                 or no `title`, a second task with a key already used, a `needs` entry naming no
                 task or the task itself, a `status` that is not a column title, a `duration` that
                 is not a positive number, a `due` (on the board or a task) that is not a date, and
                 a cycle. Each is one line, naming the task.
              3. The summary line: `ok <name>  Kanban  5 tasks in 4 rows, 3 columns, due 2026-11-13`
                 — what the board shows, so a file that parsed to nothing is visible at once.
          - title: Not checked
            body: |
              A `file` that is not there (the dialog shows no preview) and a `source` that cannot be
              read (the board's own tasks) — both are on disk beside the board, and the page's
              endpoint has no folder; the checker on a machine sees the board alone too.
  - key: authored
    label: A board is written
    trigger: chosen
    domain: Writing
    content:
      key: write
      label: Writing one
      kind: guide
      doc:
        title: Write a kanban
        description: From the template to a board with edges — and, with a due date, a calendar.
        steps:
          - key: template
            label: Start from the template
            detail: |
              `studio-check --template kanban > new.kanban`:

              ```yaml
              title: new
              due: 2026-12-31
              columns: [To do, Doing, Done]
              tasks:
                - key: first
                  title: The first thing
                  status: Doing
                  duration: 2
                - key: second
                  title: What follows it
                  needs: [first]
                  due: 2026-12-24
              ```

              Two tasks and one edge, so the rows, the schedule and a `.calendar` over it have
              something to show; replace them.
          - key: tasks
            label: Write the tasks with keys
            after: [template]
            detail: |
              `key` first, then `title` and `status` (a column title, spelled as the column is).
              Keep keys stable: edges point at them, and a rename by hand does not re-point edges
              the way the dialog does.
          - key: edges
            label: Say what needs what
            after: [tasks]
            detail: |
              `needs: [key, …]` on the task that waits. The rows follow from this alone; do not
              write a row anywhere.
          - key: dates
            label: Add the schedule's inputs
            after: [edges]
            detail: |
              `due` on the board, `duration` on tasks longer than a day, `due` on a task with a
              tighter cap. Then a `.calendar` beside it with `board: new.kanban` draws the dates.
          - key: source
            label: Source the tasks instead
            after: [template]
            detail: |
              `source: <ref>` to a `.points` file or a folder of `.node`s, and keep only `key`
              plus `needs`, `duration`, `due`, `file`, `section` in the board's rows.
          - key: check
            label: Check it
            after: [dates, source]
            detail: |
              `studio-check new.kanban` — a `needs` to no task, a `status` that is no column, a
              duplicate key, a cycle, a date or duration that is not one: each is a line naming the
              task, and the summary line counts the tasks, rows and columns the board shows.
